e('oriental_leaves_troubles','bolting_premature_flowering',{prevention:'Match sowing to the crop and variety. Pak choi generally grows best in cool, moist spring or late-summer conditions; both excessive heat and cold can trigger bolting. Protect spring seedlings from cold, keep roots moist and use bolt-resistant varieties or harvest young in summer.'},{},'Remove editorial reference to a rejected blanket August rule; retain the actual seasonal guidance.',[refs.bolting]);
e('brassica_troubles','leaf_spot_ring_spot',{description:'Brown or grey rings up to about 2.5 cm (1 in.) across appear on mature leaves. Tiny dark fruiting bodies may form concentric rings within the spots. Badly infected foliage can yellow and fall; wet conditions favour spread. Ring spot affects several leafy and heading brassicas.',applies_to:['broccoli','brussels_sprouts','cabbage','cauliflower','kale','kohl_rabi']},{description:'Brown or grey ringed spots mark older leaves; severe infection yellows foliage and causes leaf fall. Wet weather favours spread.'},'Retain approximate diagnostic size with coherent metric/imperial equivalents; broaden from Brussels sprouts to researched brassica host group.',['https://media.ahdb.org.uk/media/Default/Imported%20Publication%20Docs/Brassica%20crop%20walkers%20guide.pdf']);
e('spinach_troubles','leaf_spot',{description:'Small leaf spots, about 6 mm (¼ in.) across, may have pale brown centres and dark brown or purple margins. Centres can fall out, and spots may merge in a severe attack. Several leaf-spot diseases can look similar.'},{description:'Small spots have pale centres and dark brown or purple margins. Centres may fall out and spots merge; several diseases can look similar.'},'Convert the retained approximate quarter-inch observation coherently; avoid claiming a specific pathogen.',['https://extension.psu.edu/cercospora-leaf-spot-on-table-beets'],'Legacy spot size retained as approximate observation, not a biological limit or confirmed pathogen diagnosis.');
e('bean_and_pea_troubles','pea_moth',{description:'Pea moth caterpillars bore into developing pods and feed on peas, leaving damaged seeds and frass. Adult moths are attracted to flowering plants and usually lay eggs in June and July. Quick-maturing early or late crops can escape peak egg laying; timing depends on the season.'},{description:'Pale caterpillars feed inside pods, leaving damaged peas and frass. Flowering crops are vulnerable during summer egg laying.'},'Correct caterpillars/maggots confusion and vague spring timing; retain early-crop avoidance.',['https://www.rhs.org.uk/biodiversity/pea-moth']);
